ext_type":"Event","context_id":877,"ranking":38,"sort_order1":387.8967,"sort_order2":45.4444,"sort_order3":12.6667,"sort_order4":592,"sort_order5":385215.2553,"sort_order6":0,"matches_played":9,"matches_counted":9,"record":{"win":2,"loss":7,"tie":0}}],"alliances":{"alliances":[{"id":32433,"event_id":877,"seed":1,"teams":[11260,9974,10127]},{"id":32434,"event_id":877,"seed":2,"teams":[11228,1002,17315]},{"id":32435,"event_id":877,"seed":3,"teams":[23849,24478,10355]},{"id":32436,"event_id":877,"seed":4,"teams":[11329,19589,12087]},{"id":32437,"event_id":877,"seed":5,"teams":[20032,15993,23469]},{"id":32438,"event_id":877,"seed":6,"teams":[19207,14204,21502]}],"rankings":[]},"awards":[{"id":17388,"event_id":877,"name":"Illi(noise) Award","description":"The Chicago Robotics Invitational invites you to turn up the volume to amplify the message of FIRST! The Illi(noise) Award celebrates a team that makes the loudest impact in their community through creative media, engaging storytelling, and innovative outreach. This award recognizes teams that go beyond traditional STEM engagement, using videos, music, parodies, and other high-energy content to spread the mission of FIRST in unique and memorable ways. Whether through social media, school events, or community initiatives, these teams bring enthusiasm, originality, and passion to their advocacy, leaving a lasting impression on those they reach.","finalists":[{"place":1,"recipient":"","team_id":19589,"description":"The team that “makes robotics louder” made these judges wanna shout-er.\nThey submitted their video and we’re not kidd-io.\nThey swooped in from 2,000 miles away to snatch up the Illi(noise) award."},{"place":2,"recipient":"","team_id":20032}]},{"id":17387,"event_id":877,"name":"Star Image Award","description":"Chicago is known for one of the most iconic city flags in the world. The flag features four red stars, representing historic events in Chicago, in between two blue stripes, representing the iconic Chicago River and Lake Michigan. The four stars represent its founding with Fort Dearborn, both of its historic world’s fairs, and the great fire that burned down most of the city in 1871. You’ll see this flag all over Chicago, even embedded in our logo, in a point of civic pride like no other. Just like the Chicago flag, the Star Image Award recognizes the team with a brand that is seen throughout all aspects of their team. The team’s ROBOT, uniform, and pit have a visual image that is part of a cohesive theme that transcends a simple logo.","finalists":[{"place":1,"recipient":"","team_id":14204,"description":"This team played NO GAMES when it came to branding\nThe STARS of this award ate up the competition like Kirby\nTheir characters, colorways, and team-cohesion was a SMASH HIT"},{"place":2,"recipient":"","team_id":10138}]},{"id":17386,"event_id":877,"name":"Water Tower Award","description":"When Chicago burned down during the Great Chicago Fire in 1871, only one building was left standing among the rubble: The Water Tower. This building still stands today as a testament to the city’s perseverance in the face of adversity. This award celebrates a team that refused to give up when faced with significant and unforeseen challenges. Whether technical, logistical, personal, or environmental, this team tackled obstacles with resilience, resourcefulness, and determination. Just like the City of Chicago, which is known for its history of rebuilding stronger after adversity, this team should embody perseverance and keep pushing forward, showing what it truly means to rise against all odds. ","finalists":[{"place":1,"recipient":"","team_id":25916,"description":"Their engineering is impressive, \ntheir obstacles were oppressive, \nto these challenges they arose each glitch kept them on their toes.  \nTheir travel was severe, so they could persevere.  "},{"place":2,"recipient":"","team_id":10653}]},{"id":17385,"event_id":877,"name":"Clark Street Bridge Builders Award","description":"Chicago is well known for its sweeping river that runs through the heart of downtown and branches off to the North / South, eventually reaching the Mississippi river if one goes far enough. Thirty-seven moveable bridges span the downtown and adjacent areas, with over 300 other bridges dotting the city, making Chicago a land of many bridges. The Clark Street Bridge is a few miles south of the CRI venue and helps connect both sides of Chicago’s downtown area.The Clark Street Bridge Builders award is awarded to the team who best “builds a bridge” to connect the next generation of students to FIRST.","finalists":[{"place":1,"recipient":"","team_id":11138,"description":"This team wants to ensure the sustainability of not just their team, but their outreach as well.\nHow far can they make a bridge reach? How about 4 continents and 5 countries?\nTheir connections with others through science and technology ECLIPSES borders and oceans. "},{"place":2,"recipient":"","team_id":20744}]},{"id":17384,"event_id":877,"name":"Walter Payton Team of the Year Award","description":"In the National Football League (NFL), the Walter Payton Man of the Year Award recognizes a player who demonstrates a commitment to service, philanthropy, and impacting one’s community. Walter Payton, a long-time player for the Chicago Bears, was a superb football player and the model for giving back to one’s community. His foundation continues to give back to the City of Chicago to this day. We honor his legacy with the Walter Payton Team of the YearAward to celebrate a team’s commitment to community outreach and gracious professionalism, and to make it loud to those outside of FIRST.","finalists":[{"place":1,"recipient":"","team_id":9378,"description":"This team has poured THOUSANDS of hours into building equitable resources and STEM education with their community.\nThey built partnerships to light a FIRE in the community for science and technology\nThey have tapped into the loftiest traditions of their community to BOX UP AND SHIP innovation to every corner of their island."},{"place":2,"recipient":"","team_id":22918}]},{"id":17383,"event_id":877,"name":"Enrico Fermi Discovery Award","description":"Enrico Fermi, Nobel Laureate in Physics, created the world’s first nuclear reactor at the University of Chicago (under their football field of all places). Fermi’s first projects were in the field of statistical mechanics. Today the Fermi National Accelerator Laboratory (Fermilab) in the suburbs of Chicago continues to honor his name through its many research projects in the field of physics. In honor of his many discoveries, the Enrico Fermi Discovery Award honors a team’s dedication to and proficiency in scouting, MATCH strategy, and game analysis.","finalists":[{"place":1,"recipient":"","team_id":772,"description":"With a well-thought out, coherent, and non-confabulatory strategy, this team had a clear and perspicuous plan and could always complement their alliance partners.  This team can even select apparently aberrant or unconventional partners.  By fetching scouting data from online sources, providing real-time updates, and manually updating robot-specific performance metrics, this team is clearly heralding a golden age of FTC scouting.  While other teams are DRAGGING far behind, this team has soared ahead of the competition."},{"place":2,"recipient":"","team_id":23363}]},{"id":17382,"event_id":877,"name":"Tom Skilling Accuracy Award","description":"Chicago’s local TV station, WGN, was home to a beloved meteorologist who recently retired, Tom Skilling. He was known for adapting new technologies throughout his career for highly accurate weather forecasts, and gained a cult following over the many years he was on the air because of it. This award named in his honor recognizes a team with a highly accurate autonomous routine. While the autonomous routine doesn’t need to be the most complex or high scoring, it needs to consistently work just about every MATCH.","finalists":[{"place":1,"recipient":"","team_id":9974,"description":"This team impressed the judges by making a massive impact on the field during the autonomous period. Between scoring specimens and a custom trained vision system to detect samples, the judges marveled at the innovation and deep thought of their control systems. Their lightning-fast robot and autonomous performance really brought the hammer down on their competition. "},{"place":2,"recipient":"","team_id":12014}]},{"id":17381,"event_id":877,"name":"CTA - Creative Teleop Award","description":"Chicago’s public transit operator, the Chicago Transit Authority, can only operate the hundreds of thousands of trips it provides each day with automation working hand in hand with the operators and employees of the CTA. Our CTA, the Creative Teleop Award, similarly recognizes a team that goes above and beyond with the control of their ROBOT during the Teleop period.","finalists":[{"place":1,"recipient":"","team_id":24478,"description":"(weird pronunciation on last sentence, read ahead) This team strengthened their robot performance and their drivers remained versatile throughout the entire competition. Implementing simple yet critical controls on their robot, this team engineered their way to success through automatic commands, a lift gear shift, and voltage-based sensing that ensures the robot’s success. The robot not only LUX (pronounced lukes like luke with an S) good, it shines."},{"place":2,"recipient":"","team_id":8569}]},{"id":17380,"event_id":877,"name":"Mies Van Der Rohe “Less is More” Award","description":"The Mies Van Der Rohe “Less is More” award is named in honor of a pioneer of Modernist architecture. Ludwig Mies Van Der Rohe is well known for his work as dean of the architecture school and architect of the Illinois Institute of Technology campus, located here in Chicago, including buildings such as the world renowned S.R. Crown Hall. Utilizing simple materials or off-the-shelf products, many modernist buildings look very utilitarian at first with simple looking construction methods, but have beauty and creativity in the details. Similarly, this award recognizes teams that utilize mechanisms that are constructed with off-the-shelf components and little custom manufacturing, but still perform well in the competition.","finalists":[{"place":1,"recipient":"","team_id":19207,"description":"(shrimp puns ahead) This team boiled their bot down to pure, efficient genius. By keeping every gram of gadgetry lean, they showed the whole field that less shell-finitely is more. When it comes to sleek design, this team didn’t need a mega-prawn blueprint - they proved that staying SHRIMPLE is the secret sauce."},{"place":2,"recipient":"","team_id":11228}]},{"id":17379,"event_id":877,"name":"Loop Award","description":"Success does not occur in one step, but many iterative improvements. The Loop Award celebrates the process of building upon your previous designs to create better robots. Like a CTA train transiting from station to station, the winner must explain the various steps they took and show how those decisions led to their current robot. Ultimately this award is about the journey and not the final destination, and can be applied to a single component/feature of the ROBOT rather than the whole.","finalists":[{"place":1,"recipient":"","team_id":5064,"description":"Their build process felt like something straight out of a portal - full of unexpected turns and controlled chaos.\nBy the time they anodized the final parts, this team had turned scrap metal into artwork and a robot that looked like it was forged in a CAD-powered Renaissance\nWith each iteration, their components evolved like a deep-space telescope - getting sharper and more beautiful with every re-caliberation."},{"place":2,"recipient":"","team_id":10127}]},{"id":17378,"event_id":877,"name":"Golden Bean Award","description":"Inspired by the iconic Cloud Gate sculpture (known more commonly as The Bean) in Millenium Park, the Golden Bean award recognizes a team who brought a ROBOT with an iconic design to the Chicago Robotics Invitational. This award is looking at the overall form and function of the robot. Every aspect of the ROBOT should be well thought out and cohesive, just like the seamless mirror finish that makes up Chicago’s Bean.","finalists":[{"place":1,"recipient":"","team_id":11260,"description":"This team steered the competition with precision and style.\nTheir game was so smooth that the robotic arm looked less like a mechanism and more like someone conducting an orchestra\nThe clipper design was so slick , we thought it was writing a new game manual. "},{"place":2,"recipient":"","team_id":11329}]},{"id":17377,"event_id":877,"name":"Third Place Alliance","description":"If you see this award, please report it as a bug!","finalists":[{"place":1,"recipient":"","team_id":23849},{"place":2,"recipient":"","team_id":24478}]},{"id":17376,"event_id":877,"name":"Winning Alliance","description":"Our Winning Alliance:","finalists":[{"place":1,"recipient":"","team_id":11260},{"place":2,"recipient":"","team_id":9974},{"place":3,"recipient":"","team_id":10127}]},{"id":17375,"event_id":877,"name":"Finalist Alliance","description":"Our Finalist Alliance:","finalists":[{"place":1,"recipient":"","team_id":11329},{"place":2,"recipient":"","team_id":19589},{"place":3,"recipient":"","team_id":12087}]}],"details":{"Q1":{"id":46189,"phase":"qual","number":1,"event_id":877,"random":null,"season_score_type":"IntoTheDeepCriScore","red_score_total":591,"red_score":{"auto":156,"teleop":405,"endgame":0,"penalty":5},"red_score_details":{"auto_robot1":"NONE","auto_robot2":"NONE","auto_robot3":"NONE","auto_sample_net_near":0,"auto_sample_low_near":0,"auto_sample_high_near":2,"auto_sample_net_far":0,"auto_sample_low_far":0,"auto_sample_high_far":0,"auto_specimen_low_1":0,"auto_specimen_high_1":7,"auto_specimen_low_2":0,"auto_specimen_high_2":0,"auto_specimen_low_3":0,"auto_specimen_high_3":5,"auto_